\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{lie}

\taxon{Example}
\title{A planar Spin curve records the half-angle}

\p{Let #{Q(e_1)=Q(e_2)=1} with #{B_Q(e_1,e_2)=0}, and let
#{J(e_1)=-e_2}, #{J(e_2)=e_1}. The normalization in
\ref{fcap-0037} gives

##{b(J)=\frac12e_1e_2,
\qquad
g(\theta)=\exp\left(\frac\theta2e_1e_2\right)
=\cos\frac\theta2+\sin\frac\theta2e_1e_2.}

Since #{(e_1e_2)^2=-1}, direct multiplication gives

##{g(\theta)e_1g(\theta)^{-1}
=\cos\theta\,e_1-\sin\theta\,e_2.}

Thus differentiating at zero gives #{J} with the displayed sign, while

##{g(2\pi)=-1,\qquad g(4\pi)=1.}

The Spin parameter therefore has twice the period of its vector action.
This is the rank-two curve used in the compact-cover calculation below.}

\p{\citet{Example 3.3, p. 54}{meinrenken2013clifford} computes the same
Clifford exponential and planar action. His convention has
#{B_M(e_i,e_i)=1}, hence the same generator squares; the preceding
polar-form translation fixes the coefficient #{1/2}.}
